examples.solution_text:
$\underset{\sim}{i}$ points one unit right, so $-2\underset{\sim}{i}$ represents $2$ units left and has magnitude $2$.

$\underset{\sim}{j}$ points one unit up, so $3\underset{\sim}{j}$ represents $3$ units up and has magnitude $3$.

The horizontal and vertical directions are perpendicular; the angle between them is $90^\circ$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[->,thick,black] (0,0) -- (-2,0);
\draw[->,thick,black] (0,0) -- (0,3);
\draw[black] (-0.3,0) -- (-0.3,0.3) -- (0,0.3);
\path (-1,0) node[below] {$-2\underset{\sim}{i}$};
\path (0,1.5) node[right] {$3\underset{\sim}{j}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
Each grid square represents $1$ unit. Identify the unit vectors and give a reason.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[step=7mm,gray!40,very thin] (0,0) grid (6,4);
\draw[->,thick,black] (2,1) -- (1,1);
\draw[->,thick,black] (4,1) -- (4,2);
\draw[->,thick,black] (1,3) -- (4,3);
\path (1.5,1) node[below] {$\underset{\sim}{p}$};
\path (4,1.5) node[right] {$\underset{\sim}{q}$};
\path (2.5,3) node[above] {$\underset{\sim}{r}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
(a)

$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}8\underset{\sim}{c}-8\underset{\sim}{c}\\&=\underset{\sim}{0}\end{aligned}$

Subtracting a vector from itself gives the zero vector.

(b) The return displacement has equal magnitude and opposite direction to the outward displacement. The walker finishes at the shelter, so the total displacement is $\underset{\sim}{0}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[->,thick,black] (0,0) -- (0,4);
\draw[->,thick,black] (1.5,4) -- (1.5,0);
\path (0,2) node[left] {$4\text{ km north}$};
\path (1.5,2) node[right] {$4\text{ km south}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The two arrows show the outward and return displacements separately.
